  {
   "id": "yzs8s9xD1nMJ",
   "from": "Patrick Toche",
   "author": "b949e0841d7e094b",
   "date": "2014-01-21T12:00:36Z",
   "subject": "Re: Sending outputs to other app/client (which is not a Shiny app)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">taking bits from my apps, something like this should work:<div><br></div><div># ui.R  (say in a sidebarPanel or mainPanel or any wellPanel)</div><div><br></div><div>      list(downloadButton(&#39;downloadEPS&#39;, &quot;Save ps&quot;),downloadButton(&#39;downloadPDF&#39;, &quot;pdf&quot;))<br></div><div><div>    </div><div><br></div><div># server.R </div><div><br></div><div>    output$downloadEPS &lt;- downloadHandler(</div><div>    filename = &quot;<a href=\"http://plot.ps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://plot.ps&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792405162070000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2P8AjN5llaZS3UUQ0HXB5k\">plot.ps</a>&quot;, content = function(file) {</div><div>      postscript(file, height=6, width=8, onefile = FALSE, horizontal = FALSE)</div><div>      print(Plot())</div><div>      dev.off()</div><div>    })</div><div>    </div><div>    output$downloadPDF &lt;- downloadHandler(</div><div>    filename = &quot;plot.pdf&quot;, content = function(file) {</div><div>      pdf(file, height=6, width=8)</div><div>      print(Plot())</div><div>      dev.off()</div><div>    })    </div><div>    </div></div><div>If you&#39;re concerned about the file name, something like this could be adapted (the commented-out piece saves a cvs file, the non-commented out saves an .RData file): the point is to show that 1) you can use an observer instead of a downloadHandler, depending on how you plan the user to save things, 2) to show one way in which you can make approximately unique filenames (if that&#39;s not good enough, you can add a random string to the filename).</div><div><br></div><div><div>    # Save all answers after click on &quot;submit&quot;</div><div>    observe({</div><div>      if(is.null(input$submit) || input$submit == 0) {return()}</div><div>#      filename &lt;- paste0(&quot;answers-&quot;,input$courseName, &quot;-&quot;, input$userName, &quot;-&quot;, as.numeric(Sys.time()), &quot;.csv&quot;)</div><div>#      write.table(values$A, &#39;file&#39; = filename, &#39;sep&#39; = &quot;,&quot;, &#39;col.names&#39; = FALSE)</div><div>      filename &lt;- paste0(&quot;/data/answers-&quot;,input$courseName, &quot;-&quot;, input$userName, &quot;-&quot;, as.numeric(Sys.time()), &quot;.RData&quot;)</div><div>      save(values$A, file = filename, compress = &quot;xz&quot;)</div><div>    })</div><div>    </div></div><div>many ways you can action this, but a simple one is an actionButton (with inputId=&quot;submit&quot;, above)</div><div><div><br></div></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "vB2cXNOcAu8J",
   "from": "Viktor Rözer",
   "author": "689bc5eab59b4e7e",
   "date": "2014-01-22T11:42:49Z",
   "subject": "Re: Sending outputs to other app/client (which is not a Shiny app)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Thanks for your solution @Patrick. This definitely works for running a shiny app locally. But I am still struggling to find a way, where multiple user (let&#39;s say around 10) can use my app running on a single session on shiny-server (the free version only supports one r session at a time). Therefore I need to find a way to export the files in a way that I can allocate them to a certain user. Asking for a &#39;userName&#39; and &#39;courseName&#39; is one option, but unfortunately doesn&#39;t work for me. I would prefer some sort of user token or sessionId which I can write in the exported files metadata or in the filename.<div><br></div><div>Thanks</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ]
